.title = "Operate deployments",
.description = "Prepare, observe, and maintain an LLMD service.",
.date = .date("2026-11-06T00:00:00"),
.layout = "content.shtml",
---
Start with [Deploy LLMD with Docker]($link.page('operations/deployment')) for
image setup, readiness checks, and rollback. Use
[Monitoring and troubleshooting]($link.page('operations/monitoring')) to
inspect startup, service metrics, and accelerator state.

# [Establish a known configuration]($heading.id('establish-a-known-configuration'))

Record the server version, host operating system and architecture, accelerator
and driver, model revision, and precision. Validate that combination before
expanding deployment coverage.

# [Separate startup from readiness]($heading.id('separate-startup-from-readiness'))

Model access, storage, loading, and compilation can affect startup. A process
that is running is not sufficient evidence that requests can succeed. Confirm
readiness with a successful request using the configured model name.

# [Observe the service and its hardware]($heading.id('observe-the-service-and-its-hardware'))

Use service metrics to understand request behavior and hardware metrics to
understand resource usage. zml-smi provides hardware diagnostics; its available
backends must be checked separately from LLMD's inference support.

# [Measure changes and retain a rollback path]($heading.id('measure-changes-and-retain-a-rollback-path'))

[Tune and benchmark LLMD]($link.page('operations/benchmarking')) to compare
latency and throughput under the same workload. Preserve the previous release
and configuration so an upgrade can be reversed.

Return to the [LLMD overview]($link.page('llmd')) for server setup and client
connections.
